The L1 reader, written in L1: the three stages of the bootstrap run in order. The input is the source, a list of bytes, and the output is the quoted form of the program it reads, in the form code.luau uses. The Luau reader is the reference: for each source, this and the Luau reader should give the same expression, or both should refuse it.
The stages are imported by their paths, so the whole reader is the tokenizer, the parser and the compiler.
---
tok = boot/tokenize.prog
par = boot/parse.prog
com = boot/compile.prog
---
(fn input (let qLam (fn b [3 b ~]) (let qApp (fn (f a) [10 f a ~]) (letrec rev (fn (l acc) (if (? l) acc (rev (tail l) [(head l) acc]))) (letrec addNames (fn (ns s) (if (? ns) s (addNames (tail ns) [(head ns) s]))) (letrec wrapVals (fn (vs body) (if (? vs) body (wrapVals (tail vs) (qApp (qLam body) (head vs))))) (let src (head input) (let names (head (tail input)) (let values (tail (tail input)) (wrapVals (rev values ~) (com [(par (tok src)) (addNames names ~)])))))))))))
---
["42" ~ ~] => [0 42 ~] fuel 10000000
["(fn x x)" ~ ~] => [3 [2 0 ~] ~] fuel 10000000
["((fn x x) 4)" ~ ~] => [10 [3 [2 0 ~] ~] [0 4 ~] ~] fuel 10000000
["(let x 1 x)" ~ ~] => [10 [3 [2 0 ~] ~] [0 1 ~] ~] fuel 10000000
["x" (list "x") (list [0 1 ~])] => [10 [3 [2 0 ~] ~] [0 1 ~] ~] fuel 10000000
["(f 1" ~ ~] => error fuel 10000000
["(f 1))" ~ ~] => error fuel 10000000
["x" ~ ~] => error fuel 10000000
